<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Channel Islands</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Channel Islands are an archipelago off the coast of Normandy, comprising the Bailiwicks of Jersey and Guernsey, the latter including Alderney and Sark. Once part of the Duchy of Normandy, they stayed with the English crown after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> lost mainland Normandy in 1204. They are British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crown-dependencies">Crown Dependencies</a>, not part of the United Kingdom, and were the only British territory occupied by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/channel-islands.jpg" alt="Channel Islands" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Copernicus Sentinel-2, ESA · CC BY-SA 3.0 igo · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Channel_Islands_by_Sentinel-2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Jersey, the largest of the <b>Channel Islands</b> at about 118 square kilometres, lies some 22 kilometres off the Cotentin peninsula of Normandy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bailiwick-of-jersey">Bailiwick of Jersey · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Channel Islands</b> are the last part of the old Duchy of Normandy still held by the British crown, and their law still rests partly on Norman custom.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-normandy">Duchy of Normandy · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Sark in the <b>Channel Islands</b>, settled in 1565 by Helier de Carteret under a charter from Elizabeth I and divided into forty tenements whose holders sat in its parliament, kept what was called Europe’s last feudal system until 2008.</blockquote><a href="/government/feudalism">Feudalism · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Like the other <b>Channel Islands</b> it remained a possession of the crown in its role as Duke of Normandy, never part of the Kingdom of England.</blockquote><a href="/state/alderney">Alderney</a></li></ul></section>
